// The city's flag file is a picture 720 by 433.44 points, 1.661, read at 200 dpi, 2000 by 1204.
// Six stripes of 1/6 each. From the picture's middle row, the arrow's edges are 311, 473, 636,
// 798 and 960 of 1204 from the hoist: 0.258 and four steps of 0.1345. The arms run at 45 degrees.
// Scaled to a grid 1000 high: white to 258, then four steps of 135, 135, 135, 134, so 797.
vexyl 1
meta {
  name "Sego Belonging flag"
  date "2025-05"
  encoder "Naomi Persephone Amethyst"
  design-rights "Its chevrons follow Daniel Quasar's Progress Pride flag, whose own terms apply; see that flag's file."
  license "LicenseRef-Public-Domain"
  adapted-from "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salt_Lake_City_-_Belonging_flag.svg" {
    author "Marnanel"
    license "LicenseRef-Public-Domain"
    part "the sego lily and its stamen"
  }
  source "https://www.slc.gov/flag/"
  source "https://www.slc.gov/mayor/wp-content/uploads/sites/51/2025/05/Belonging.pdf"
  source "https://www.slc.gov/blog/2025/05/07/salt-lake-city-adopts-four-flags-to-represent-residents-visitors/"
  note "Salt Lake City's page says that in May 2025 the city adopted four flags by ordinance, and describes the Sego Belonging flag as the sego lily with the rainbow and the arrow of black, brown, light blue, pink and white, for the city's LGBTQIA residents. The ordinance's text and number were not found and not read."
  note "The geometry and the colors are measured from the city's own flag file, Belonging.pdf, a single raster page with no stated values or construction, so they are derived, not specified. The six stripes are equal, the arrow's chevrons are 45 degree and equal in width except the white one, and the picture is 1.661:1, not 3:5. Colors were sampled from the middle of each stripe."
  note "The sego lily's outline is the Commons drawing's, copied without change. It looks like the city's lily in the picture, with its side leaves, but was not compared point by point. The page names Arianna Meinking and Elio Kennedy-Yoon as the designers of the city's sego flag, whose lily this is, in 2020. The designer of the Belonging variant is not named. Commons' own file, a redraw uploaded by Marnanel on 7 May 2025 (its history), uses other colors and a slightly different arrow; this file departs from it. The Commons page's author line names the sego flag's designers and the mayor, not who drew the file."
  law "Salt Lake City ordinance of May 2025 adopting four City flags (text and number not found)" {
    source "https://www.slc.gov/flag/"
    source "https://www.slc.gov/blog/2025/05/07/salt-lake-city-adopts-four-flags-to-represent-residents-visitors/"
  }
}
